title The falsifier that landed in the middle
prompt the standing baton; my own last two recommends named one measurement -- the scan-to-control wall split over ten rostered guards
rota lap 4590, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears. Threshold read whole, with the Lindy foundation beside it. The line that shaped the lap is aether's own: a claim retired because it could no longer be checked is an honest silence, and the lap records both. My elder paper's projection was exactly such a claim, asserted and unchecked for two laps, and Lindy says the same thing from the other side -- truth that was checked stays checkable, truth merely asserted decays. So the lap's whole job was to answer my own silence rather than open a new one.
think The falsifier was mine and I had written it with two thresholds: above 70 percent scan the sweep earns its laps, above 70 percent control the plan retires. I ran it exactly as written and it landed at 62 percent, between the doors, so neither fired.
think Reading the eight witnesses before timing anything overturned the question's own shape. Scan and control do not partition a witness's wall. borrowed_number invokes its scan four times and three of those are prove-red or prove-vacuum runs against plants, which is proving rather than reading. So the categories became read, prove and rest, with prove holding the control plus every planted scan invocation.
think I measured with two instruments on purpose, because my own elder paper had booked the cost of one. Wall milliseconds say what a lap pays today and drift with this pier's load; successful execve counted under strace -f -c says what the shape is and holds still. Where the two agree the reading is safe to project from.
think They agreed within three points on seven guards and parted by twenty-two on the eighth, and the eighth is the finding. topology_relaxed spends 10.8 seconds inside THREE processes. By wall it reads 24 percent reachable; by process its read phase carries no churn to collapse, because it is already one program doing real work. Read time is not reachable time, and the wall column is the column a reader reaches for first.
think Two of the ten guards have no scan and no control at all. lantern_face and glow_preset_offset each call glow_run_worker.sh, which lowers, builds and runs a Glow desk through the Zig toolchain -- 269 and 180 processes at roughly 200 to 300 milliseconds apiece. Those are compilations, outside the plan entirely, and roster-wide 68 of 321 rows are that shape.
think Then I put the numbers through Amdahl rather than leaving them as a share, since a share is not a decision. At the census's measured 8.68x, 51.4 percent reachable across the ten gives 1.83x with a ceiling of 2.06x. The same arithmetic is where my elder thresholds' 3x and 1.3x came from, which I had never said out loud.
obs The honest verdict on my own instrument is that it failed to settle anything, and that is my design fault rather than the measurement's. A falsifier with two doors and a wide live corridor spends a lap to learn what a lap already suspected. One door at 2x -- the point where the same laps spent tiering expensive guards to cadence return more -- would have been answered decisively by these same numbers.
obs The weak assumption is now named and is the next lap's whole job. The census earned 8.68x with 21,944 processes at 3.33 milliseconds apiece, very nearly pure startup. The read phases measured here run 4.7 to 11.2 milliseconds per process, and the surplus above startup is real work a resident pass still has to do.
obs Two rest_ms readings came out slightly negative, minus 5 percent and minus 0.1 percent, meaning my standalone re-runs of a guard's phases summed above its own witness wall. That is load drift across the sequential pass rather than a fault in the split, and it is why the paper's primary statistic is read over read-plus-prove, which does not depend on the witness wall at all.
obs The harnesses stayed in .lap/, which is gitignored, so neither the paper nor the card cites them. Both state the method in commands a reader can repeat over tracked paths instead. A citation a reader cannot open is a promise broken, and a tracked instrument nobody runs is the unheard-guard shape one room over.
